WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.280
Picture a massive piece of modern infrastructure, like, let's say, a huge suspension bridge spanning this really wide, treacherous river.

00:09.420 --> 00:14.000
Okay, yeah, I can picture that. Lots of steel cables, massive concrete pillars.

00:14.320 --> 00:26.620
Exactly. And when you look at it, there's this, you know, this profound expectation of absolute precision because engineering, to most of us, is this very binary, highly visible thing.

00:26.700 --> 00:29.100
Right. I mean, you look at the sensors and they're all reading normal.

00:29.100 --> 00:38.200
Yeah. The load-bearing metrics on the digital dashboard are solid green. The morning commute traffic is flowing smoothly. Everything looks perfect.

00:38.420 --> 00:44.860
And the structural engineer points to the data and says, with, like, complete confidence, the bridge is perfectly safe.

00:44.960 --> 00:56.140
And we believe them because we are completely conditioned to trust the measurable present. But what if our obsession with measuring the present is exactly what's blinding us to catastrophic failures in the future?

00:56.140 --> 01:01.740
What's fascinating here is that that is the terrifying question at the absolute heart of the research we are looking at today.

01:02.040 --> 01:07.700
Okay, let's unpack this. Because if you're joining us for this deep dive right now, you know we don't just stop at the surface.

01:07.900 --> 01:16.000
We really don't. Because beneath that comforting dashboard of green lights, the reality of complex systems is, well, it's infinitely more murky.

01:16.000 --> 01:22.820
Yeah. So imagine if we peer down to the molecular level of that bridge right into the steel itself.

01:23.040 --> 01:28.940
Right. And there might be a microscopic crack beginning to form in a critical support strut. Just a tiny, tiny fissure.

01:29.100 --> 01:39.480
Exactly. And we are going to take that microscopic crack and use it to completely dismantle and, honestly, rewire how you think about reality, intelligence, and problem solving.

01:39.480 --> 01:45.640
Because today we are looking at a really paradigm-shifting 2026 paper by the researcher Fliction.

01:45.940 --> 01:52.360
Yeah, it's titled, Reality is What Can Be Reached. Reachability, Identification, and the Limits of Extraction.

01:53.020 --> 02:03.200
And our mission for this deep dive is to move your understanding away from a wild build out of static objects and toward a world constructed entirely out of what the author calls accessible futures.

02:03.200 --> 02:12.840
Which, I mean, it's a complete paradigm shift. It challenges the core ontological assumptions of modern science, of statistics, and definitely artificial intelligence.

02:13.120 --> 02:20.380
So let's go back to that bridge analogy, because Fliction uses it to expose this massive flaw in how we evaluate information.

02:21.120 --> 02:23.440
Let's look closely at that microscopic crack.

02:23.440 --> 02:36.740
Right. By every single standard metric we currently use to evaluate the health of that bridge-like vibration analysis, expected forecast loss, average load calculations, that crack is mathematically invisible.

02:37.160 --> 02:42.080
Invisible. Because it's so small that it doesn't meaningfully change the weight of the bridge, right?

02:42.080 --> 02:51.660
Exactly. It doesn't alter the aerodynamics. I mean, if you have a digital sensor on that beam, the change in structural tension is so minuscule, it just gets swallowed up as a rounding error.

02:51.660 --> 02:54.060
So it's essentially just treated as noise by the system.

02:54.140 --> 02:58.520
It is literally treated as noise. You know, our models are built to compress data.

02:58.780 --> 03:05.240
They minimize the description length of the information we store because, well, we can't possibly track every single atom in a bridge.

03:05.720 --> 03:06.800
That would be impossible.

03:07.920 --> 03:14.580
So a compression algorithm summarizing the state of the bridge looks at that tiny anomaly and just discards it.

03:14.580 --> 03:21.460
Yeah, it discards it because it contributes almost zero information to our understanding of the bridge's observable behavior right now.

03:21.460 --> 03:30.800
But, and this is where it gets really crazy, the paper argues that this tiny, statistically insignificant anomaly matters enormously.

03:31.080 --> 03:35.800
It does. And the reason it matters completely reorients our entire definition of significance.

03:35.800 --> 03:42.440
Right, because it doesn't matter because of its current physical size, and it doesn't matter because of its contribution to the present data set.

03:42.440 --> 03:46.480
No, it matters because it sits at a branching point of possible futures.

03:46.780 --> 03:49.180
Wow. A branching point.

03:49.280 --> 03:52.240
Yeah, the crack represents a fork in the road of reality.

03:52.520 --> 03:56.560
From this exact present state, multiple potential futures diverge.

03:56.560 --> 04:02.900
So like, in one trajectory, the load distributes differently, and you just get years of normal, continued use?

04:03.000 --> 04:12.740
Exactly. But in another trajectory, the crack propagates slowly, eventually requiring a multi-million dollar repair project that shuts down traffic for months.

04:12.940 --> 04:14.280
And then there's the nightmare trajectory.

04:14.280 --> 04:26.280
Right, the third trajectory, where the right combination of high winds, heavy traffic, and maybe thermal contraction hits that compromise strut, and it leads to catastrophic structural failure.

04:26.740 --> 04:30.220
So the significance of the crack has nothing to do with its present footprint.

04:30.760 --> 04:36.860
Its significance is defined entirely by its capacity to alter which futures remain accessible to the bridge.

04:36.860 --> 04:44.040
You nailed it. And Fliction provides a huge wealth of parallel examples to show that this isn't just some niche engineering problem.

04:44.460 --> 04:48.620
This dynamic exists across literally every complex domain.

04:49.100 --> 04:52.720
Yeah. Let's talk about software engineering, because that one is super relatable.

04:52.820 --> 05:01.320
Oh, absolutely. You can have a latent fault, like a tiny logical error buried in millions of lines of code that just sits completely dormant for years.

05:01.320 --> 05:06.980
Right. It passes every unit test. It causes zero latency. It's completely invisible to ordinary metrics.

05:07.060 --> 05:14.260
Until, you know, a very specific, totally unpredictable sequence of user inputs occurs, hitting that exact edge case.

05:14.400 --> 05:17.880
And boom, suddenly you have a global server outage that costs billions of dollars.

05:18.360 --> 05:24.700
Exactly. The bug was statistically insignificant until the exact moment it destroyed the system's future.

05:24.920 --> 05:27.160
And we see the exact same thing in biology, too, right?

05:27.160 --> 05:36.080
We do. Imagine a population of millions of organisms, and a single genetic mutation occurs in just one individual.

05:36.380 --> 05:43.340
Statistically speaking, if you are measuring the total biomass, or the current genetic variants of the entire population,

05:43.920 --> 05:47.400
that single mutation is, like, negligible variants.

05:47.500 --> 05:50.580
Yeah, you would smooth it right out of your data. You'd call it noise.

05:50.900 --> 05:55.300
But that specific mutation might grant immunity to a novel environmental pressure.

05:55.300 --> 06:03.300
Right. Meaning that single anomaly will completely redirect the evolutionary trajectory of the entire species across geological time.

06:03.420 --> 06:10.400
Or think about epidemiology. Like, patient zero of a novel pathogen in a really remote, isolated population.

06:10.560 --> 06:16.120
That's a great example. If you look at global health metrics, one sick person out of eight billion is a statistical zero.

06:16.320 --> 06:18.140
It doesn't even register on any dashboard.

06:18.440 --> 06:22.700
But we all know now that this single data point holds the keys to a global pandemic.

06:22.700 --> 06:27.720
Yeah. And fliction even extends this logic to economics and politics.

06:28.460 --> 06:30.660
Think of a massive corporate balance sheet.

06:30.840 --> 06:32.400
Okay, so like a Fortune 500 company.

06:32.600 --> 06:40.480
Exactly. There might be a small, legally ambiguous financial liability just buried in the footnotes of some subsidiary's tax return.

06:40.740 --> 06:46.940
And under ordinary accounting metrics, calculating present liquidity and asset valuation, it's just invisible.

06:46.940 --> 06:56.900
Completely invisible. But if macroeconomic conditions shift in a very specific way, that tiny liability triggers a cascade of defaults that bankrupts the entire conglomerate.

06:57.000 --> 06:59.080
That is terrifying. What about the politics example?

06:59.320 --> 07:04.300
Oh, in international relations, it could be a subtle cultural misunderstanding between two diplomats.

07:04.880 --> 07:08.420
It persists unnoticed, causing zero current conflict.

07:08.540 --> 07:12.680
Until it acts as the unseen catalyst for a large-scale war years later.

07:12.680 --> 07:13.300
Exactly.

07:13.660 --> 07:16.800
So when you lay it all out like that, a really chilling pattern emerges here.

07:17.360 --> 07:30.160
All of our most advanced, celebrated evaluation frameworks, like measuring variance explained in statistics, or error reduction in machine learning, or cost-benefit analysis in business, they all assess importance based on the present moment.

07:30.460 --> 07:35.800
They do. They essentially just ask, how much does this data point impact my current model?

07:36.320 --> 07:41.700
So wait, are we systematically blinding ourselves to the very things that actually dictate the future?

07:41.700 --> 07:51.240
According to this framework, yes, we are completely blind. And this exposes a massive systemic limitation in our modern intellectual culture.

07:51.520 --> 07:55.440
Because we evaluate importance by looking at present contribution.

07:55.800 --> 08:06.020
Right. But what determines the significance of the crack in the bridge, or the latent software bug, isn't the amount of information it contributes to a current description.

08:06.020 --> 08:16.260
Because a detail might contribute zero data to your present spreadsheet, while simultaneously holding absolute control over the boundary between radically different futures.

08:16.380 --> 08:19.980
It's like we're optimizing for an entirely wrong dimension of reality.

08:20.220 --> 08:26.820
Yeah. We are so obsessed with creating high-fidelity maps of what is, that we are completely ignoring what could be.

08:26.900 --> 08:30.380
Infliction goes as far as to say this is a fundamental ontological problem.

08:30.380 --> 08:36.200
Okay. Ontology, that's the branch of philosophy that deals with the nature of existence, right? Like what things actually are.

08:36.520 --> 08:48.920
Exactly. From Aristotle to Newton, right up to modern artificial intelligence, we have always assumed that reality consists primarily of objects, states, facts, and entities.

08:49.300 --> 08:53.200
We assume the universe is basically a giant container populated with things.

08:53.200 --> 08:59.780
Yes. And the job of science, therefore, is to measure, categorize, predict, and compress those things.

09:00.300 --> 09:04.620
We view the crack as just another property of an object to be measured and weighed.

09:05.060 --> 09:13.420
But if our current metrics are actively deceiving us, then just building better sensors or collecting more data about the objects won't actually fix the problem.

09:13.420 --> 09:23.200
No, it won't. We actually need an entirely new theory of reality. We have to stop looking at a world made of objects and start looking at a world made of possibilities.

09:23.540 --> 09:25.840
Which brings us to what the paper calls the Great Inversion.

09:26.300 --> 09:30.540
Right. The Great Inversion fundamentally redefines reality as reachability.

09:30.920 --> 09:34.520
Okay. To understand this, let's contrast it with the traditional view first.

09:34.520 --> 09:39.620
So, the traditional intellectual hierarchy assumes that reality comes first, and it is made of objects.

09:40.000 --> 09:45.960
Those objects have relations with each other. They bump into each other. They chemically react. They exchange information.

09:46.500 --> 09:49.900
And eventually, over time, those relations generate futures.

09:50.320 --> 09:58.440
Exactly. In this view, objects are the bedrock. Futures are just a downstream consequence, like an eventual byproduct of what objects do.

09:58.440 --> 10:04.760
And that makes intuitive sense to most of us. The bridge is made of steel and concrete. Those are the objects.

10:05.160 --> 10:10.260
Whatever happens to that steel tomorrow, whether it rusts or collapses or stays standing, that's the future.

10:10.420 --> 10:13.220
Right. The object exists first. The future happens to it later.

10:13.660 --> 10:17.020
But Fliction proposes that we have this entirely backward.

10:17.320 --> 10:23.780
Completely backward. The new proposed hierarchy places reality as fundamentally a reachability structure.

10:23.780 --> 10:30.140
And from that underlying structure of possibilities, objects emerge as derivative constructs.

10:30.140 --> 10:30.460
Yes.

10:30.580 --> 10:37.920
Wait, really? So, reality is the structure of possibilities first, and the physical objects we see and touch are essentially secondary.

10:38.160 --> 10:39.900
Like, they emerge from the possibilities.

10:40.340 --> 10:41.720
That is the core argument, yeah.

10:41.980 --> 10:46.560
Instead of treating reality as a collection of entities that just happen to possess possibilities,

10:46.880 --> 10:50.940
we must treat reality as a vast geometry of accessible transformations.

10:51.260 --> 10:51.620
Oh, wow.

10:51.620 --> 10:57.160
Objects aren't the bedrock. They are just stabilized patterns within that deeper geometry.

10:57.440 --> 11:00.680
Okay, let's slow down and really unpack the mechanics of reachability here,

11:00.740 --> 11:04.100
because this is the foundational concept for the rest of our deep dive.

11:04.620 --> 11:06.560
How does the paper formally define it?

11:07.060 --> 11:10.380
Well, the formal definition in the text uses a bit of mathematical notation,

11:10.580 --> 11:12.440
but the concept itself is very elegant.

11:13.120 --> 11:16.800
Let's refer to the current state of any system as X.

11:16.800 --> 11:21.960
Okay, so X could be the state of the bridge, the state of a chessboard, or the state of your bank account.

11:22.040 --> 11:22.480
Exactly.

11:23.220 --> 11:29.800
Fliction defines reachability as the complete set of all possible futures that can be reached from that state X.

11:30.480 --> 11:30.740
Oh, no.

11:31.260 --> 11:34.560
This is denoted mathematically as capital R of X.

11:34.940 --> 11:41.040
The critical shift here is that the object of primary theoretical interest is no longer the state X itself,

11:41.260 --> 11:44.340
but the shape and structure of capital R of X.

11:44.340 --> 11:49.980
So the meaning of a state, the actual identity of it, is inseparable from its future horizon.

11:50.100 --> 11:50.480
Yes.

11:51.320 --> 11:56.820
You literally cannot define what a thing is without defining what it can become.

11:56.920 --> 11:58.580
That is the perfect way to articulate it.

11:58.620 --> 12:01.560
I mean, if you have two things that look completely identical on the surface,

12:01.800 --> 12:07.740
like our bridge with the microscopic crack, and a perfectly pristine bridge right next to it.

12:07.760 --> 12:10.740
But they lead to completely different accessible futures.

12:10.920 --> 12:11.140
Right.

12:11.260 --> 12:13.540
Then they are fundamentally different things.

12:13.540 --> 12:15.400
Their R of X is different.

12:15.800 --> 12:15.820
Okay.

12:15.980 --> 12:17.000
And conversely.

12:17.100 --> 12:17.540
Conversely.

12:17.620 --> 12:21.720
If you have two things that are physically different, maybe one bridge is painted red and

12:21.720 --> 12:27.680
the other is painted blue, but they permit the exact same future transformations and load-bearing

12:27.680 --> 12:31.120
trajectories, they are functionally the exact same thing.

12:31.180 --> 12:34.600
Because what matters isn't what the state is, but what the state allows.

12:35.280 --> 12:35.800
Exactly.

12:35.800 --> 12:38.680
What matters isn't what the state is, but what the state allows.

12:38.680 --> 12:40.700
That is a massive mental pivot.

12:41.340 --> 12:45.480
But, you know, the paper points out that this kind of thinking isn't entirely science fiction.

12:45.640 --> 12:49.720
It's actually bubbling up independently in several different scientific disciplines.

12:50.060 --> 12:50.700
It really is.

12:51.240 --> 12:56.440
Fliction synthesizes evidence from various fields to show that this inversion is already implicitly

12:56.440 --> 12:58.080
driving advanced research.

12:58.220 --> 12:59.180
Like in mathematics, right?

12:59.180 --> 12:59.300
Yeah.

12:59.520 --> 13:00.940
Take dynamical systems theory.

13:01.800 --> 13:07.320
When mathematicians analyze the long-term behavior of a complex system like fluid turbulence

13:07.320 --> 13:11.880
or planetary orbits, they don't focus on isolated momentary states.

13:12.220 --> 13:13.020
What do they focus on?

13:13.180 --> 13:16.440
They analyze things called attractors and basins of attraction.

13:16.440 --> 13:22.300
The specific state of the system at any given microsecond matters far less than the qualitative

13:22.300 --> 13:25.280
structure of the space of trajectories emanating from it.

13:25.700 --> 13:28.300
So they are literally studying the geometry of the future.

13:28.620 --> 13:29.140
Precisely.

13:29.260 --> 13:33.720
The paper also leans heavily on ecology, which I think provides a really visceral example.

13:34.080 --> 13:36.700
Ecology is perhaps the most intuitive domain for this.

13:37.360 --> 13:43.580
Think about the concept of a keystone species, like sea otters in a Pacific kelp forest ecosystem.

13:43.580 --> 13:47.620
A keystone species doesn't matter primarily because of its physical properties.

13:48.120 --> 13:52.780
An ecologist doesn't value the otter just because of its body mass, its metabolic rate,

13:52.880 --> 13:54.380
or, you know, the density of its fur.

13:54.660 --> 13:54.860
Right.

13:55.240 --> 14:00.240
The otter matters profoundly because of the pathways it sustains through the entire ecosystem.

14:00.420 --> 14:00.800
Exactly.

14:01.220 --> 14:05.720
Its significance is determined entirely by what its removal would foreclose.

14:05.860 --> 14:09.480
Because if you remove the otter, the sea urchin population explodes.

14:09.480 --> 14:13.940
And then the urchins consume, the kelp holdfasts, the kelp forest collapses,

14:14.420 --> 14:18.520
and the entire habitat for hundreds of other species just vanishes.

14:19.000 --> 14:21.700
So the object, the otter, is basically just a placeholder

14:21.700 --> 14:25.320
for the vast reachability structure of the kelp forest future.

14:25.720 --> 14:28.420
Yeah, its true value is the futures it preserves.

14:28.620 --> 14:30.940
We see the exact same logic in finance, too.

14:31.580 --> 14:33.460
The paper mentions options trading.

14:33.600 --> 14:35.840
Oh, options trading is a perfect parallel.

14:35.840 --> 14:39.340
Because when you buy a call or put an option on the stock market,

14:39.540 --> 14:43.200
you aren't actually buying a physical barrel of oil or a share of a company.

14:43.480 --> 14:43.520
Right.

14:43.680 --> 14:47.140
You are literally purchasing the right to take a specific action in the future.

14:47.400 --> 14:52.620
The entire monetary value of that contract derives from the accessibility of future states

14:52.620 --> 14:53.500
it preserves for you.

14:54.100 --> 14:58.740
You are, quite literally, pricing the geometry of accessible futures.

14:59.040 --> 15:00.480
That's a really sharp way to put it.

15:00.720 --> 15:03.960
Actually, I want to reframe this for you, the listener, to make it incredibly practical.

15:03.960 --> 15:06.200
Think about your own life trajectory.

15:07.020 --> 15:10.380
Think about why you might decide to pursue a demanding college degree,

15:10.640 --> 15:15.240
or why you might spend a grueling six months learning a complex new skill,

15:15.360 --> 15:17.340
like a programming language or public speaking.

15:17.460 --> 15:18.900
It's a huge investment of time.

15:19.120 --> 15:19.360
Right.

15:19.920 --> 15:23.060
But the value of that degree isn't the physical piece of paper.

15:23.620 --> 15:28.180
And the value of the skill isn't just the raw encyclopedia of facts you memorized.

15:28.180 --> 15:35.100
The true underlying value is the entirely new set of future career paths it opens up for you.

15:35.540 --> 15:41.600
Because before you had that degree, certain futures, certain jobs, networks, income brackets

15:41.600 --> 15:43.140
were completely walled off.

15:43.240 --> 15:43.940
They were unreachable.

15:44.500 --> 15:50.800
But by acquiring the skill, your personal R of X to your reachability structure has permanently expanded.

15:51.040 --> 15:53.180
You weren't just accumulating facts in your brain.

15:53.320 --> 15:55.480
You were purchasing access to new futures.

15:55.480 --> 15:59.160
That is such a brilliant translation of the math into human experience.

15:59.660 --> 16:03.360
And if we follow the logical implications of your example,

16:03.460 --> 16:05.920
it fundamentally changes the definition of knowledge itself.

16:06.040 --> 16:06.380
How so?

16:06.540 --> 16:12.360
Well, we have historically thought of knowledge as achieving perfect descriptive fidelity of the world.

16:12.500 --> 16:19.300
Like a map is considered good if it perfectly captures every single topographic detail of the terrain.

16:19.580 --> 16:24.620
But from a reachability perspective, knowledge isn't about descriptive accuracy at all.

16:24.620 --> 16:27.540
No, it's about tracking future accessibility.

16:28.180 --> 16:31.840
So a representation succeeds not when it looks exactly like the world,

16:32.080 --> 16:36.660
but when it preserves the distinctions that determine which futures remain possible.

16:36.900 --> 16:37.220
Yes.

16:37.720 --> 16:40.140
A map that shows you exactly where the bridge is cracked,

16:40.580 --> 16:42.660
allowing you to avoid a catastrophic route,

16:42.980 --> 16:47.800
is infinitely more knowledgeable than a photorealistic satellite image of the bridge

16:47.800 --> 16:50.960
that compresses the crack into just a smooth pixel.

16:50.960 --> 16:51.600
Wow.

16:51.940 --> 16:54.400
If knowledge is fundamentally about tracking the future,

16:54.800 --> 16:58.500
then we need to severely interrogate basically everything we currently associate

16:58.500 --> 17:00.880
with artificial intelligence and strategic planning.

17:00.960 --> 17:01.800
We really do.

17:02.020 --> 17:04.140
Because when we look at how the tech industry builds AI,

17:04.420 --> 17:06.480
or how corporations design automated systems,

17:06.880 --> 17:08.360
there is an unquestioned assumption

17:08.360 --> 17:11.340
that activities like predicting, planning, and optimizing

17:11.340 --> 17:13.900
are the absolute bedrock of intelligence.

17:13.900 --> 17:16.680
And this brings us to a highly controversial,

17:17.040 --> 17:20.160
but deeply fascinating section of Fliction's paper,

17:20.600 --> 17:21.640
The Foundation of Action.

17:22.080 --> 17:23.100
Okay, let's get into this.

17:23.220 --> 17:25.720
The author makes the audacious claim that prediction,

17:26.240 --> 17:28.900
planning, control, optimization, and safety

17:28.900 --> 17:32.280
are not foundational concepts of intelligence at all.

17:32.340 --> 17:34.540
Which sounds completely counterintuitive,

17:34.720 --> 17:37.580
because entire multi-billion dollar industries,

17:37.740 --> 17:39.700
I'm talking self-driving cars,

17:39.700 --> 17:42.880
algorithmic trading, supply chain logistics,

17:43.440 --> 17:46.880
are built entirely around optimizing those exact things.

17:47.260 --> 17:49.340
They treat optimization as the holy grail.

17:49.460 --> 17:50.260
They absolutely do.

17:50.400 --> 17:52.440
But Fliction meticulously points out

17:52.440 --> 17:54.200
that every single one of those activities

17:54.200 --> 17:57.420
logically presupposes a pre-existing space

17:57.420 --> 17:58.660
of accessible futures.

17:58.880 --> 18:01.200
So they are entirely dependent on a structure

18:01.200 --> 18:03.940
that they themselves lack the capacity to create.

18:04.020 --> 18:04.500
Exactly.

18:04.660 --> 18:06.360
They are passengers, not the vehicle.

18:06.360 --> 18:09.080
Let's walk through this logical dependency chain

18:09.080 --> 18:10.640
step-by-step, starting with prediction.

18:11.020 --> 18:13.460
How is prediction downstream of reachability?

18:13.720 --> 18:15.980
Well, to predict is to make a definitive statement

18:15.980 --> 18:18.120
about a future state of some system.

18:18.700 --> 18:20.600
You are pointing to a specific outcome.

18:20.940 --> 18:23.180
But before a prediction can even be evaluated

18:23.180 --> 18:24.520
as correct or incorrect,

18:25.020 --> 18:27.460
there must already exist some underlying

18:27.460 --> 18:29.560
defined geometry of possibilities

18:29.560 --> 18:31.880
that determines which futures are available

18:31.880 --> 18:33.440
to be predicted in the first place.

18:33.720 --> 18:34.180
Ah.

18:34.180 --> 18:37.740
So prediction is essentially just picking a point on a map.

18:37.880 --> 18:38.360
Exactly.

18:38.740 --> 18:42.140
It presupposes that the map of accessibility already exists.

18:42.420 --> 18:44.720
And planning has to follow the exact same logic.

18:45.060 --> 18:47.540
You can't plan a route if the roads haven't been drawn.

18:47.740 --> 18:48.240
Precisely.

18:48.640 --> 18:51.520
Planning requires multiple futures to select among.

18:51.920 --> 18:54.820
A planning algorithm evaluates alternative trajectories,

18:54.960 --> 18:57.540
like if I take route A, it takes 20 minutes.

18:57.840 --> 18:59.580
If I take route B, it takes 30 minutes

18:59.580 --> 19:00.920
and compares their consequences.

19:00.920 --> 19:03.660
But the planner doesn't generate those alternative trajectories

19:03.660 --> 19:04.300
out of thin air.

19:04.540 --> 19:04.960
No.

19:05.100 --> 19:08.100
It operates exclusively within a space of possibilities

19:08.100 --> 19:10.600
whose structural boundaries are already given to it.

19:10.780 --> 19:11.940
And what about control theory?

19:12.520 --> 19:14.580
Like an autopilot maintaining altitude?

19:15.020 --> 19:17.480
Or a factory robot assembling a car?

19:17.480 --> 19:20.900
A controller seeks to guide a system towards some desired,

19:21.300 --> 19:23.080
stable region of a state's space,

19:23.620 --> 19:25.440
usually compensating for disturbances.

19:25.980 --> 19:28.460
But again, it navigates a pre-existing geometry.

19:28.780 --> 19:30.720
It doesn't define what the space is.

19:30.720 --> 19:32.720
Which brings us to optimization.

19:32.720 --> 19:35.920
And man, this feels like the heaviest blow

19:35.920 --> 19:40.160
because modern machine learning, economics, decision theory,

19:40.780 --> 19:43.260
they all effectively define intelligent behavior

19:43.260 --> 19:46.220
as maximizing some objective function

19:46.220 --> 19:49.180
or reward over a space of possible states.

19:49.420 --> 19:51.680
Reinforcement learning is entirely based on this.

19:51.960 --> 19:52.080
Right.

19:52.220 --> 19:54.260
And this is exactly where the hidden dependency

19:54.260 --> 19:55.760
becomes actively dangerous.

19:56.360 --> 19:58.620
An optimizer is essentially just a search algorithm.

19:58.820 --> 20:00.000
It searches a given space

20:00.000 --> 20:02.260
and ranks futures by their objective value,

20:02.700 --> 20:03.760
hunting for the highest score.

20:03.880 --> 20:04.120
Okay.

20:04.300 --> 20:06.160
But it takes absolutely no responsibility

20:06.160 --> 20:09.200
for defining what counts as a valid alternative in that space.

20:09.320 --> 20:09.580
Bingo.

20:09.920 --> 20:12.500
It requires a geometry of possibilities to exist

20:12.500 --> 20:14.960
before it can execute a single line of code.

20:14.960 --> 20:18.240
So if you hand an optimizer a flawed map of possibilities,

20:18.500 --> 20:21.500
it will perfectly flawlessly execute a terrible plan.

20:21.780 --> 20:24.640
The paper introduces a deeply chilling concept here.

20:25.320 --> 20:28.980
A system that is optimizing the correct objective

20:28.980 --> 20:30.820
within the wrong representation.

20:31.060 --> 20:31.520
Oh, man.

20:32.040 --> 20:34.300
Because in the discourse around AI safety,

20:34.460 --> 20:35.820
we spend a massive amount of time

20:35.820 --> 20:38.660
worrying about AI optimizing the wrong objective.

20:38.760 --> 20:39.020
Right.

20:39.120 --> 20:41.000
The classic sci-fi thought experiment

20:41.000 --> 20:42.560
of the paperclip maximizer.

20:42.600 --> 20:42.720
Yeah.

20:42.780 --> 20:44.080
You tell an AI to make paperclips,

20:44.460 --> 20:47.040
and it decides to harvest the iron in human blood

20:47.040 --> 20:48.500
to make more paperclips

20:48.500 --> 20:51.400
because you didn't explicitly specify human safety.

20:51.800 --> 20:53.600
The objective was misaligned.

20:54.300 --> 20:55.860
But Fliction argues a deeper,

20:56.140 --> 20:58.460
far more insidious possibility exists.

20:58.460 --> 21:01.480
Your objective might be perfectly morally aligned.

21:01.600 --> 21:02.500
You might tell the AI,

21:02.780 --> 21:04.320
build the bridge safely and cheaply.

21:04.820 --> 21:07.260
But if the map of reality the system is using

21:07.260 --> 21:08.980
is fundamentally flawed-like,

21:09.300 --> 21:10.720
if its underlying ontology

21:10.720 --> 21:13.300
has compressed the microscopic crack into noise,

21:13.940 --> 21:16.140
the optimizer cannot detect this blindness

21:16.140 --> 21:18.340
from within its own representational world.

21:18.580 --> 21:18.900
Wow.

21:19.340 --> 21:22.380
So it is faithfully, vigorously pursuing a good goal

21:22.380 --> 21:25.460
inside a geometry that has already deleted something essential.

21:25.460 --> 21:26.880
So what you're saying is that

21:26.880 --> 21:28.660
all the safety guardrails in the world

21:28.660 --> 21:30.220
are mathematically useless

21:30.220 --> 21:33.820
if the AI's foundational map of reality

21:33.820 --> 21:35.620
has already deleted the difference

21:35.620 --> 21:37.400
between a safe outcome and a lethal one.

21:37.660 --> 21:39.360
That is exactly the implication.

21:39.940 --> 21:42.720
And Fliction formalizes this in the appendices

21:42.720 --> 21:45.560
with a proof called the guardrail incompleteness theorem.

21:45.820 --> 21:46.460
Guardrail incompleteness.

21:46.460 --> 21:47.180
Yeah.

21:47.620 --> 21:49.560
It demonstrates that safety isn't a feature

21:49.560 --> 21:52.280
you can just bolt onto the outside of a system after the fact.

21:52.580 --> 21:55.520
It is a foundational representational requirement.

21:55.760 --> 21:57.700
Let's create a really concrete analogy for this

21:57.700 --> 21:58.740
to make sure we're getting it.

21:59.680 --> 22:03.560
Imagine we are building an autonomous medical diagnostic AI.

22:03.560 --> 22:05.660
Okay, let's look at the logic there.

22:06.180 --> 22:08.620
In the process of creating its representation of the world,

22:08.780 --> 22:11.800
this diagnostic AI has to simplify things.

22:12.040 --> 22:14.280
It can't process every atom of a patient's body,

22:14.380 --> 22:15.520
so it categorizes symptoms.

22:15.980 --> 22:17.160
It collapses data.

22:17.420 --> 22:17.680
Makes sense.

22:17.980 --> 22:19.960
Now, suppose during that initial collapse,

22:20.000 --> 22:21.860
it looks at two different blood panel results.

22:22.340 --> 22:24.660
One indicates a benign common cold.

22:25.220 --> 22:27.220
The other indicates the early stages

22:27.220 --> 22:30.540
of a rare, highly aggressive autoimmune failure.

22:30.540 --> 22:34.220
Because the blood markers look statistically very similar

22:34.220 --> 22:35.100
in the present moment,

22:35.600 --> 22:37.980
the AI's compression algorithm merges them

22:37.980 --> 22:39.780
into the exact same category.

22:40.100 --> 22:42.640
It labels them both minor viral infection.

22:42.920 --> 22:45.180
So it has just collapsed the uncracked bridge

22:45.180 --> 22:47.020
and the cracked bridge into the same state.

22:47.180 --> 22:47.740
Precisely.

22:47.860 --> 22:50.620
Now, a team of human safety engineers comes along later.

22:51.180 --> 22:54.700
They review the AI and try to impose a rigid safety guardrail

22:54.700 --> 22:56.660
on top of its decision-making matrix.

22:57.040 --> 22:58.620
They program a strict rule.

22:58.620 --> 23:00.600
Never prescribe treatment X

23:00.600 --> 23:02.620
if there is any sign of autoimmune failure

23:02.620 --> 23:04.440
because it will be fatal.

23:04.920 --> 23:07.660
But the system literally cannot see the difference anymore.

23:08.020 --> 23:10.400
The distinction between benign and fatal

23:10.400 --> 23:13.560
was destroyed at the foundational level of projection.

23:13.740 --> 23:14.140
Exactly.

23:14.520 --> 23:16.560
The safety guardrail operates downstream

23:16.560 --> 23:18.480
way above the level of representation.

23:19.000 --> 23:21.520
So the guardrail inherits the blindness of the map

23:21.520 --> 23:23.740
without any capacity to compensate for it.

23:23.860 --> 23:25.960
So the AI looks at the autoimmune patient,

23:26.380 --> 23:27.980
registers minor viral infection,

23:27.980 --> 23:29.900
sees no conflict with the guardrail,

23:30.240 --> 23:31.900
and just administers the fatal treatment.

23:32.020 --> 23:32.280
Yes.

23:32.620 --> 23:35.640
The theorem proves that no downstream safety mechanism

23:35.640 --> 23:38.540
can ever recover a distinction that was deleted upstream.

23:39.040 --> 23:40.080
That is profound.

23:40.700 --> 23:44.060
It implies that the most dangerous consequential action

23:44.060 --> 23:45.920
any intelligence system ever takes

23:45.920 --> 23:47.760
isn't making a decision,

23:48.020 --> 23:49.140
or moving an actuator,

23:49.240 --> 23:50.240
or executing a trade.

23:50.380 --> 23:50.660
No.

23:50.660 --> 23:53.500
It's deciding what to forget in the first place.

23:54.420 --> 23:57.120
Every single time we create a representation,

23:57.520 --> 23:58.400
or define a category,

23:58.540 --> 23:59.360
or build a concept,

23:59.640 --> 24:02.140
we are performing an irreversible act

24:02.140 --> 24:04.420
of what the author calls identification.

24:04.920 --> 24:06.420
Which is the perfect bridge

24:06.420 --> 24:09.300
to the next major philosophical pillar of the paper.

24:09.700 --> 24:11.620
The idea that every ontology

24:11.620 --> 24:13.980
is fundamentally a theory of identification.

24:13.980 --> 24:16.940
This revolves around a mathematical concept

24:16.940 --> 24:18.900
known as the projection problem.

24:19.020 --> 24:20.920
Let's unpack the math here conversationally.

24:21.220 --> 24:23.960
Fliction explains that every representation we use,

24:24.020 --> 24:25.220
whether it's a scientific law,

24:25.420 --> 24:26.580
an AI neural network,

24:26.760 --> 24:28.180
or just a human word force

24:28.180 --> 24:29.260
as an equivalence relation.

24:29.440 --> 24:29.680
Right.

24:29.860 --> 24:31.780
The paper uses the mathematical notation

24:31.780 --> 24:32.520
of a projection,

24:33.020 --> 24:35.000
denoted as pi maps x to m.

24:35.260 --> 24:36.160
But in plain English,

24:36.320 --> 24:37.600
think of x as the massive,

24:37.760 --> 24:39.720
overwhelmingly complex physical reality.

24:39.780 --> 24:39.920
Yeah.

24:39.920 --> 24:41.380
And m is our much smaller,

24:41.640 --> 24:43.200
simpler mental model or map.

24:43.480 --> 24:45.160
The projection is the act

24:45.160 --> 24:46.720
of mapping the huge reality

24:46.720 --> 24:47.900
into the small model.

24:48.560 --> 24:49.620
And in doing so,

24:49.920 --> 24:51.520
we are forced to declare

24:51.520 --> 24:54.520
distinct physical states to be the same.

24:54.740 --> 24:56.200
Humans do this constantly.

24:56.380 --> 24:57.180
I mean, we wouldn't be able

24:57.180 --> 24:58.840
to process reality without it.

24:58.900 --> 25:00.120
When you walk into a furniture store,

25:00.220 --> 25:02.760
you see a hundred different wooden structures.

25:03.300 --> 25:04.300
Some have four legs,

25:04.420 --> 25:05.080
some have three,

25:05.460 --> 25:06.060
some are oak,

25:06.180 --> 25:06.800
some are pine,

25:07.380 --> 25:08.940
some are upholstered in red velvet.

25:08.940 --> 25:10.640
Others are bare wood.

25:11.080 --> 25:13.220
But your brain instantaneously projects

25:13.220 --> 25:15.220
all of that massive physical diversity

25:15.220 --> 25:16.700
into a single,

25:16.900 --> 25:19.160
highly compressed mental category.

25:19.600 --> 25:19.880
Chair.

25:20.040 --> 25:20.520
Exactly.

25:20.740 --> 25:22.300
You purposefully collapse

25:22.300 --> 25:23.600
the distinctions between them

25:23.600 --> 25:24.800
just to navigate the room.

25:25.060 --> 25:25.760
If someone asked me

25:25.760 --> 25:26.820
why I call all of those

25:26.820 --> 25:28.160
different things the same name,

25:28.640 --> 25:29.680
the traditional justification

25:29.680 --> 25:31.260
is simply similarity.

25:31.720 --> 25:32.040
I'd say,

25:32.260 --> 25:33.120
well, they look alike.

25:33.240 --> 25:34.340
They share similar features.

25:34.620 --> 25:35.760
But Sliction argues

25:35.760 --> 25:37.520
that appealing to similarity

25:37.520 --> 25:39.700
is an entirely circular argument.

25:39.700 --> 25:40.140
Really?

25:40.820 --> 25:41.280
How so?

25:41.540 --> 25:43.020
You only recognize things as similar

25:43.020 --> 25:44.920
if you have already implicitly decided

25:44.920 --> 25:45.900
which features matter

25:45.900 --> 25:46.680
and which don't.

25:46.800 --> 25:47.260
Oh, I see.

25:47.400 --> 25:49.700
To declare two vastly different pieces

25:49.700 --> 25:51.580
of furniture are similar chairs,

25:52.160 --> 25:53.200
you've already decided

25:53.200 --> 25:54.460
that the color of the wood

25:54.460 --> 25:55.720
or the exact placement

25:55.720 --> 25:56.940
of a scratch on the leg

25:56.940 --> 25:58.140
is irrelevant noise,

25:58.340 --> 26:00.420
while the presence of a flat seat

26:00.420 --> 26:01.600
is essential data.

26:01.600 --> 26:03.360
Therefore, ontology,

26:03.760 --> 26:04.800
our grand theory

26:04.800 --> 26:06.060
of what exists in the universe,

26:06.480 --> 26:07.920
is not actually a catalog

26:07.920 --> 26:08.660
of what exists.

26:08.820 --> 26:10.240
It is, at its core,

26:10.840 --> 26:12.120
a theory of what can be

26:12.120 --> 26:12.880
safely ignored.

26:13.120 --> 26:14.320
A theory of what can be

26:14.320 --> 26:15.120
safely ignored.

26:15.380 --> 26:16.880
That completely inverts

26:16.880 --> 26:17.520
how we are taught

26:17.520 --> 26:18.660
to think about science.

26:18.880 --> 26:19.520
We are taught

26:19.520 --> 26:21.120
science is the pursuit

26:21.120 --> 26:22.920
of capturing every detail.

26:23.420 --> 26:25.040
But the paper details

26:25.040 --> 26:26.820
these brilliant historical successes

26:26.820 --> 26:28.640
of collapsing distinctions

26:28.640 --> 26:30.240
to prove that science

26:30.240 --> 26:31.520
thrives on forgetting.

26:32.540 --> 26:33.300
The history of physics

26:33.300 --> 26:34.440
is essentially a history

26:34.440 --> 26:36.180
of highly successful forgetting.

26:36.720 --> 26:38.240
Consider Newtonian mechanics.

26:38.380 --> 26:38.600
Okay.

26:38.840 --> 26:39.900
Newton's laws of motion

26:39.900 --> 26:41.160
are incredibly powerful,

26:41.300 --> 26:42.160
not because they capture

26:42.160 --> 26:42.840
every nuance

26:42.840 --> 26:43.860
of the physical world,

26:44.040 --> 26:45.340
but because they aggressively

26:45.340 --> 26:46.640
collapse the enormous

26:46.640 --> 26:47.560
chaotic diversity

26:47.560 --> 26:48.880
of physical trajectories

26:48.880 --> 26:51.380
into a few elegant equations.

26:51.660 --> 26:52.420
Like when calculating

26:52.420 --> 26:53.360
the arc of a cannonball.

26:53.680 --> 26:54.000
Right.

26:54.440 --> 26:55.300
Newtonian physics

26:55.300 --> 26:56.080
often tells us

26:56.080 --> 26:57.300
to completely ignore

26:57.300 --> 26:58.020
air resistance

26:58.020 --> 26:59.700
wind and the microscopic

26:59.700 --> 27:01.220
surface texture of the ball.

27:01.420 --> 27:02.440
It treats those

27:02.440 --> 27:03.700
as safely ignorable.

27:04.120 --> 27:04.980
Or thermodynamics,

27:05.540 --> 27:06.180
which might be

27:06.180 --> 27:07.040
the ultimate example

27:07.040 --> 27:08.200
of successful forgetting.

27:08.480 --> 27:08.880
Absolutely.

27:09.240 --> 27:10.200
Think about the steam

27:10.200 --> 27:11.100
inside an engine.

27:11.800 --> 27:12.420
There are roughly

27:12.420 --> 27:13.480
10 to the power

27:13.480 --> 27:14.920
of 23 individual

27:14.920 --> 27:16.360
water molecules in there,

27:16.780 --> 27:17.400
bouncing around

27:17.400 --> 27:18.320
at different speeds,

27:18.760 --> 27:19.740
constantly colliding.

27:20.000 --> 27:21.420
Tracking the exact position

27:21.420 --> 27:22.100
and velocity

27:22.100 --> 27:23.420
of every single molecule

27:23.420 --> 27:25.100
is computationally impossible.

27:25.440 --> 27:26.600
So thermodynamics

27:26.600 --> 27:28.000
completely ignores

27:28.000 --> 27:28.980
the uncountable

27:28.980 --> 27:30.020
individual microscopic

27:30.020 --> 27:30.980
projectories.

27:31.620 --> 27:32.780
It forcibly collapses

27:32.780 --> 27:33.800
all that complexity

27:33.800 --> 27:35.100
into aggregate

27:35.100 --> 27:37.140
macroscopic state variables

27:37.140 --> 27:37.960
like temperature,

27:38.220 --> 27:39.400
pressure, and volume.

27:39.780 --> 27:40.360
It treats billions

27:40.360 --> 27:41.760
of distinct molecular arrangements

27:41.760 --> 27:43.080
as completely equivalent

27:43.080 --> 27:44.080
as long as they produce

27:44.080 --> 27:45.220
the same overall pressure.

27:45.380 --> 27:45.640
Yes.

27:45.800 --> 27:46.680
And we do the same thing

27:46.680 --> 27:47.620
with fluid mechanics.

27:48.080 --> 27:49.060
When we model

27:49.060 --> 27:50.100
the flow of a river

27:50.100 --> 27:51.580
or aerodynamic lift

27:51.580 --> 27:52.660
over an airplane wing,

27:52.840 --> 27:53.980
we treat the water

27:53.980 --> 27:54.720
or the air

27:54.720 --> 27:56.920
as a continuous smooth medium.

27:56.920 --> 27:58.480
We completely forget

27:58.480 --> 27:59.580
that it is actually

27:59.580 --> 28:00.720
composed of discrete

28:00.720 --> 28:01.920
chaotic particles.

28:02.560 --> 28:03.180
We collapse

28:03.180 --> 28:04.500
microscopic reality

28:04.500 --> 28:05.920
into density gradients.

28:06.280 --> 28:07.780
And these abstractions

28:07.780 --> 28:08.380
have driven

28:08.380 --> 28:09.720
centuries of

28:09.720 --> 28:11.000
technological progress.

28:11.220 --> 28:11.900
They succeed

28:11.900 --> 28:13.000
phenomenally.

28:13.200 --> 28:13.860
But they succeed

28:13.860 --> 28:14.740
not because

28:14.740 --> 28:16.080
they preserve

28:16.080 --> 28:16.960
all information,

28:17.640 --> 28:18.680
but because they manage

28:18.680 --> 28:19.280
to preserve

28:19.280 --> 28:20.240
the right information

28:20.240 --> 28:21.780
for their specific domain

28:21.780 --> 28:23.080
while ruthlessly

28:23.080 --> 28:24.240
deleting the rest.

28:24.500 --> 28:25.140
It's strategic,

28:25.640 --> 28:26.660
successful forgetting.

28:26.960 --> 28:27.640
But that brings us

28:27.640 --> 28:29.020
directly to the dark side

28:29.020 --> 28:30.060
of this process,

28:30.440 --> 28:31.300
what Fliction calls

28:31.300 --> 28:32.500
the projection problem.

28:32.860 --> 28:34.220
Because we evaluate

28:34.220 --> 28:35.140
our AI models

28:35.140 --> 28:36.180
and scientific theories

28:36.180 --> 28:36.880
based on what

28:36.880 --> 28:38.140
they successfully capture,

28:38.480 --> 28:40.020
but we rarely audit

28:40.020 --> 28:41.000
what they are deleting.

28:41.260 --> 28:42.020
And as we saw

28:42.020 --> 28:42.820
with the guardrail

28:42.820 --> 28:43.960
incompleteness theorem,

28:44.220 --> 28:44.960
that deletion

28:44.960 --> 28:46.060
is irreversible.

28:46.060 --> 28:47.020
Once you map

28:47.020 --> 28:47.980
two distinct

28:47.980 --> 28:48.960
physical realities

28:48.960 --> 28:50.420
to the exact same point

28:50.420 --> 28:51.700
in your mathematical model,

28:52.120 --> 28:52.720
you cannot

28:52.720 --> 28:53.420
unmap them.

28:53.620 --> 28:54.000
The emissions

28:54.000 --> 28:54.900
are the necessary

28:54.900 --> 28:56.520
price of abstraction itself.

28:56.860 --> 28:57.400
And because those

28:57.400 --> 28:58.320
emissions are literally

28:58.320 --> 28:59.440
invisible from within

28:59.440 --> 28:59.900
the model,

29:00.300 --> 29:00.920
the model doesn't

29:00.920 --> 29:01.740
know what it doesn't know.

29:02.180 --> 29:02.660
If you forget

29:02.660 --> 29:03.400
the wrong thing,

29:03.540 --> 29:04.180
you are doomed.

29:04.360 --> 29:04.620
Exactly.

29:04.680 --> 29:05.560
If your abstraction

29:05.560 --> 29:07.000
categorizes the cracked

29:07.000 --> 29:07.840
bridge and the

29:07.840 --> 29:08.720
uncracked bridge

29:08.720 --> 29:09.720
as the same state,

29:10.340 --> 29:11.100
your model will

29:11.100 --> 29:12.040
confidently predict

29:12.040 --> 29:12.980
safety right up

29:12.980 --> 29:13.700
until the catastrophic

29:13.700 --> 29:14.200
collapse.

29:14.520 --> 29:15.500
It will never see

29:15.500 --> 29:15.940
it coming,

29:16.260 --> 29:17.120
because the necessary

29:17.120 --> 29:18.180
data was left out

29:18.180 --> 29:18.700
of the universe

29:18.700 --> 29:19.540
you built for it.

29:19.800 --> 29:20.820
Which brings us

29:20.820 --> 29:21.500
to the most

29:21.500 --> 29:22.540
critical operational

29:22.540 --> 29:23.280
question of the

29:23.280 --> 29:24.160
entire paper.

29:24.780 --> 29:25.480
If similarity

29:25.480 --> 29:27.060
is a circular trap

29:27.060 --> 29:27.820
and predicting

29:27.820 --> 29:28.640
the present data

29:28.640 --> 29:29.800
is a flawed metric,

29:30.520 --> 29:31.440
how do we actually

29:31.440 --> 29:32.320
know if we've forgotten

29:32.320 --> 29:33.080
the right things

29:33.080 --> 29:34.020
or the wrong things?

29:34.160 --> 29:34.340
Right.

29:34.420 --> 29:35.120
How do we evaluate

29:35.120 --> 29:36.060
the quality of an

29:36.060 --> 29:36.560
abstraction?

29:37.240 --> 29:38.300
Fliction provides

29:38.300 --> 29:39.380
a new gold standard

29:39.380 --> 29:40.160
for evaluation,

29:40.640 --> 29:41.560
a concept called

29:41.560 --> 29:42.480
admissibility.

29:42.740 --> 29:43.500
Let's break down

29:43.500 --> 29:44.360
exactly what an

29:44.360 --> 29:45.700
admissible projection is.

29:45.700 --> 29:47.120
The paper states

29:47.120 --> 29:47.680
that a projection

29:47.680 --> 29:48.420
is admissible

29:48.420 --> 29:49.880
if, and only if,

29:50.360 --> 29:51.220
whenever it declares

29:51.220 --> 29:52.180
two states to be

29:52.180 --> 29:52.600
equivalent,

29:53.120 --> 29:53.960
those two states

29:53.960 --> 29:54.980
possess the exact

29:54.980 --> 29:55.860
same reachability

29:55.860 --> 29:56.400
structure.

29:56.700 --> 29:57.720
Mathematically speaking,

29:57.880 --> 29:59.120
capital R of X,

29:59.260 --> 30:00.020
one must equal

30:00.020 --> 30:01.060
capital R of X,

30:01.140 --> 30:01.320
two.

30:01.580 --> 30:02.280
So let's translate

30:02.280 --> 30:03.020
that into concrete

30:03.020 --> 30:03.420
terms.

30:04.360 --> 30:06.140
If your mental model

30:06.140 --> 30:07.600
or your AI algorithm

30:07.600 --> 30:08.680
looks at state A

30:08.680 --> 30:09.460
and state B

30:09.460 --> 30:09.920
and says,

30:10.120 --> 30:11.360
these are functionally

30:11.360 --> 30:12.240
the same thing,

30:13.180 --> 30:14.480
it is only an admissible

30:14.480 --> 30:15.760
model if the futures

30:15.760 --> 30:17.200
accessible from state A

30:17.200 --> 30:18.360
are perfectly identical

30:18.360 --> 30:19.040
to the futures

30:19.040 --> 30:20.320
accessible from state B.

30:20.620 --> 30:20.940
Yes.

30:21.380 --> 30:22.100
If your model

30:22.100 --> 30:23.460
collapses two states

30:23.460 --> 30:24.780
together whose future

30:24.780 --> 30:25.740
horizons actually

30:25.740 --> 30:26.820
differ in reality,

30:27.100 --> 30:28.400
then it has destroyed

30:28.400 --> 30:29.520
a physical distinction

30:29.520 --> 30:30.620
that has genuine

30:30.620 --> 30:31.760
bearing on what the

30:31.760 --> 30:32.820
system can subsequently

30:32.820 --> 30:33.300
do.

30:33.480 --> 30:34.340
It has blinded you

30:34.340 --> 30:34.880
to a future.

30:35.080 --> 30:36.420
That is an inadmissible

30:36.420 --> 30:36.960
collapse.

30:37.080 --> 30:37.480
To make this

30:37.480 --> 30:38.460
incredibly clear,

30:38.860 --> 30:40.340
let's contrast the two

30:40.340 --> 30:41.320
distinct types of

30:41.320 --> 30:42.460
representational error

30:42.460 --> 30:43.140
you can make

30:43.140 --> 30:44.380
when building a model.

30:44.480 --> 30:45.520
Okay, let's categorize

30:45.520 --> 30:46.400
them as type 1

30:46.400 --> 30:47.220
and type 2

30:47.220 --> 30:48.380
representational errors.

30:48.940 --> 30:49.900
In a type 1 error,

30:50.320 --> 30:50.840
your projection

30:50.840 --> 30:52.040
merges two states

30:52.040 --> 30:52.900
that look physically

30:52.900 --> 30:53.820
different in the present

30:53.820 --> 30:54.620
but actually share

30:54.620 --> 30:55.460
the exact same

30:55.460 --> 30:56.500
future possibilities.

30:56.760 --> 30:57.480
So this represents

30:57.480 --> 30:58.660
imperfect information.

30:59.240 --> 30:59.860
Your map is a bit

30:59.860 --> 31:00.200
blurry,

31:00.460 --> 31:01.400
it lost some details

31:01.400 --> 31:01.980
about the present

31:01.980 --> 31:02.820
aesthetic of the world,

31:03.100 --> 31:04.060
but it is completely

31:04.060 --> 31:04.420
safe.

31:04.640 --> 31:05.220
You haven't altered

31:05.220 --> 31:05.720
the geometry

31:05.720 --> 31:06.380
of the future.

31:06.660 --> 31:07.480
To go back to our

31:07.480 --> 31:07.880
bridge,

31:08.240 --> 31:09.120
a type 1 error

31:09.120 --> 31:10.460
is like a large

31:10.460 --> 31:11.560
cosmetic scratch

31:11.560 --> 31:12.280
on the paint

31:12.280 --> 31:13.440
of the suspension cable.

31:13.440 --> 31:14.440
The scratched

31:14.440 --> 31:15.120
bridge and the

31:15.120 --> 31:15.920
unscratched bridge

31:15.920 --> 31:16.600
have the exact

31:16.600 --> 31:17.420
same structural

31:17.420 --> 31:18.320
futures available

31:18.320 --> 31:18.800
to them.

31:19.000 --> 31:19.520
Collapsing them

31:19.520 --> 31:20.220
in your engineering

31:20.220 --> 31:21.120
model and treating

31:21.120 --> 31:21.840
them as the same

31:21.840 --> 31:23.140
bridge is perfectly

31:23.140 --> 31:23.580
safe.

31:23.840 --> 31:24.500
It's an admissible

31:24.500 --> 31:25.000
abstraction.

31:25.140 --> 31:25.640
Precisely.

31:25.860 --> 31:27.040
But a type 2 error

31:27.040 --> 31:27.880
is the nightmare

31:27.880 --> 31:28.460
scenario.

31:29.020 --> 31:29.580
This is when you

31:29.580 --> 31:30.760
merge two states

31:30.760 --> 31:31.740
that look visually

31:31.740 --> 31:32.520
or statistically

31:32.520 --> 31:33.480
identical in the

31:33.480 --> 31:34.540
present but actually

31:34.540 --> 31:35.420
have radically

31:35.420 --> 31:36.420
diverging futures.

31:36.760 --> 31:37.300
This is the

31:37.300 --> 31:38.620
microscopic crack

31:38.620 --> 31:39.440
in the foundational

31:39.440 --> 31:39.920
support.

31:39.920 --> 31:41.300
The observable

31:41.300 --> 31:41.900
difference in the

31:41.900 --> 31:42.780
present is tiny.

31:43.460 --> 31:44.020
The informational

31:44.020 --> 31:45.140
loss during compression

31:45.140 --> 31:45.840
is mathematically

31:45.840 --> 31:47.260
negligible, but the

31:47.260 --> 31:48.160
effect on the future

31:48.160 --> 31:48.860
is enormous.

31:49.720 --> 31:50.500
Merging the cracked

31:50.500 --> 31:51.120
and uncracked

31:51.120 --> 31:52.280
states is an

31:52.280 --> 31:52.980
inadmissible

31:52.980 --> 31:53.920
catastrophic collapse.

31:54.780 --> 31:55.520
So admissibility

31:55.520 --> 31:56.220
is a framework

31:56.220 --> 31:57.200
designed exclusively

31:57.200 --> 31:58.020
to detect and

31:58.020 --> 31:58.900
avoid type 2

31:58.900 --> 31:59.300
errors.

31:59.460 --> 32:00.560
But the paper

32:00.560 --> 32:01.420
acknowledges that

32:01.420 --> 32:02.020
in the messy

32:02.020 --> 32:02.800
real world,

32:03.300 --> 32:04.100
nothing is ever

32:04.100 --> 32:04.920
perfectly binary.

32:05.640 --> 32:06.700
We rarely have

32:06.700 --> 32:08.180
perfect r of x1

32:08.180 --> 32:09.320
equals r of x2

32:09.320 --> 32:09.860
equality.

32:10.480 --> 32:10.900
So fliction

32:10.900 --> 32:11.620
introduces a

32:11.620 --> 32:12.540
continuous mathematical

32:12.540 --> 32:13.340
measure for this,

32:13.420 --> 32:13.540
right?

32:13.940 --> 32:14.620
Yes, a metric

32:14.620 --> 32:15.820
called admissibility

32:15.820 --> 32:17.320
distortion, denoted

32:17.320 --> 32:18.360
as capital D

32:18.360 --> 32:19.680
subscript A of pi.

32:20.160 --> 32:20.720
Instead of just

32:20.720 --> 32:21.400
saying a model

32:21.400 --> 32:22.140
is valid or

32:22.140 --> 32:23.300
invalid, this

32:23.300 --> 32:24.020
formula measures

32:24.020 --> 32:25.180
exactly how much

32:25.180 --> 32:26.140
future accessibility

32:26.140 --> 32:27.220
is systematically

32:27.220 --> 32:28.200
destroyed by a

32:28.200 --> 32:29.020
given abstraction.

32:29.500 --> 32:30.240
So it calculates

32:30.240 --> 32:30.920
the expected

32:30.920 --> 32:31.860
divergence between

32:31.860 --> 32:32.960
the future horizons

32:32.960 --> 32:34.220
of pairs of states

32:34.220 --> 32:35.320
that the projection

32:35.320 --> 32:36.060
has forcibly

32:36.060 --> 32:36.740
grouped together.

32:36.940 --> 32:37.900
In essence, it is

32:37.900 --> 32:38.520
a mathematical

32:38.520 --> 32:39.280
measurement of

32:39.280 --> 32:40.260
destructive forgetting.

32:40.440 --> 32:40.980
And there is a

32:40.980 --> 32:42.040
terrifying formal

32:42.040 --> 32:43.240
proof attached to

32:43.240 --> 32:44.080
this concept in the

32:44.080 --> 32:45.060
text, labeled

32:45.060 --> 32:46.180
theorem 1,

32:46.980 --> 32:48.000
monotonicity under

32:48.000 --> 32:48.540
composition.

32:49.340 --> 32:50.580
That theorem is

32:50.580 --> 32:51.280
the point of no

32:51.280 --> 32:52.180
return for system

32:52.180 --> 32:52.600
design.

32:53.000 --> 32:53.980
It proves mathematically

32:53.980 --> 32:55.020
that once an

32:55.020 --> 32:56.180
inadmissible collapse

32:56.180 --> 32:56.900
happens at the

32:56.900 --> 32:58.060
base, foundational

32:58.060 --> 32:58.760
level of your

32:58.760 --> 33:00.120
model, once a tiny

33:00.120 --> 33:01.260
bit of admissibility

33:01.260 --> 33:01.940
distortion is

33:01.940 --> 33:03.120
introduced, no

33:03.120 --> 33:03.540
amount of

33:03.540 --> 33:04.560
sophisticated processing

33:04.560 --> 33:05.340
downstream can

33:05.340 --> 33:05.800
undo it.

33:06.080 --> 33:06.840
The distortion is

33:06.840 --> 33:08.060
monotone, meaning

33:08.060 --> 33:08.560
it strictly

33:08.560 --> 33:09.220
preserves or

33:09.220 --> 33:09.980
increases the

33:09.980 --> 33:10.220
error.

33:10.380 --> 33:10.660
Yes.

33:10.900 --> 33:11.860
It only stays the

33:11.860 --> 33:12.500
same or gets

33:12.500 --> 33:13.460
worse as you add

33:13.460 --> 33:14.420
more layers, more

33:14.420 --> 33:15.400
algorithms, or more

33:15.400 --> 33:16.120
guardrails to the

33:16.120 --> 33:16.360
model.

33:16.360 --> 33:17.500
Let's use an analogy

33:17.500 --> 33:18.700
from digital media to

33:18.700 --> 33:19.620
visualize this

33:19.620 --> 33:20.560
irreversibility.

33:21.400 --> 33:22.440
Imagine taking a

33:22.440 --> 33:23.920
massive, uncompressed,

33:24.380 --> 33:25.580
super high-resolution

33:25.580 --> 33:26.440
raw photograph.

33:26.680 --> 33:26.840
Yeah.

33:27.540 --> 33:28.300
Somewhere in those

33:28.300 --> 33:29.220
millions of pixels,

33:29.640 --> 33:30.460
there is a tiny,

33:30.600 --> 33:31.260
almost invisible,

33:31.460 --> 33:32.980
digital watermark that

33:32.980 --> 33:34.420
proves who owns the

33:34.420 --> 33:34.800
copyright.

33:35.800 --> 33:36.640
Now you need to

33:36.640 --> 33:37.760
email the photo, so

33:37.760 --> 33:38.940
you save it as a

33:38.940 --> 33:39.560
highly compressed

33:39.560 --> 33:40.160
JPEG.

33:40.240 --> 33:40.900
Right, to save

33:40.900 --> 33:41.360
space.

33:41.480 --> 33:41.760
Exactly.

33:42.120 --> 33:42.620
The compression

33:42.620 --> 33:43.460
algorithm looks at

33:43.460 --> 33:44.500
the data, tries to

33:44.500 --> 33:45.260
save space, and

33:45.260 --> 33:46.520
says, hey, these

33:46.520 --> 33:47.820
slight variations in

33:47.820 --> 33:48.720
pixel color don't

33:48.720 --> 33:49.520
matter for the

33:49.520 --> 33:50.140
overall human

33:50.140 --> 33:50.880
perception of the

33:50.880 --> 33:52.140
image, and it

33:52.140 --> 33:52.980
averages them out.

33:53.240 --> 33:54.040
It deletes the

33:54.040 --> 33:54.860
watermark to save

33:54.860 --> 33:55.180
memory.

33:55.360 --> 33:55.920
That act of

33:55.920 --> 33:56.620
deletion is the

33:56.620 --> 33:57.140
inadmissible

33:57.140 --> 33:57.660
collapse.

33:57.760 --> 33:58.060
Exactly.

33:58.360 --> 33:59.540
And monotonicity

33:59.540 --> 34:00.540
under composition

34:00.540 --> 34:01.580
dictates that once

34:01.580 --> 34:02.700
the image is a JPEG,

34:02.760 --> 34:03.440
you can't fix it

34:03.440 --> 34:03.680
later.

34:04.000 --> 34:04.780
You can't run it

34:04.780 --> 34:05.280
through a million

34:05.280 --> 34:05.880
dollar Photoshop

34:05.880 --> 34:07.580
filter, or upscale

34:07.580 --> 34:08.140
it with the most

34:08.140 --> 34:09.100
advanced generative

34:09.100 --> 34:10.640
AI, and expect to

34:10.640 --> 34:11.280
find the original

34:11.280 --> 34:12.120
watermark again.

34:12.220 --> 34:12.920
The data is

34:12.920 --> 34:13.820
mathematically gone

34:13.820 --> 34:14.460
from the universe

34:14.460 --> 34:15.100
of that file.

34:15.680 --> 34:16.340
No downstream

34:16.340 --> 34:17.160
processing can

34:17.160 --> 34:17.860
recover what was

34:17.860 --> 34:18.660
destroyed upstream.

34:19.140 --> 34:19.720
That is a

34:19.720 --> 34:20.700
flawless analogy.

34:21.380 --> 34:21.860
The future

34:21.860 --> 34:23.100
structure, the

34:23.100 --> 34:23.980
ability to prove

34:23.980 --> 34:24.960
ownership, has been

34:24.960 --> 34:26.100
permanently deleted

34:26.100 --> 34:26.980
by the compression.

34:27.200 --> 34:27.880
Which naturally

34:27.880 --> 34:28.800
forces us to

34:28.800 --> 34:29.680
confront a highly

34:29.680 --> 34:30.940
uncomfortable reality

34:30.940 --> 34:31.580
about modern

34:31.580 --> 34:32.160
technology.

34:32.160 --> 34:33.460
If the vital

34:33.460 --> 34:34.580
data is gone, and

34:34.580 --> 34:35.100
our current

34:35.100 --> 34:36.580
evaluation metrics

34:36.580 --> 34:37.340
like predictive

34:37.340 --> 34:38.280
accuracy or

34:38.280 --> 34:39.180
compression rates or

34:39.180 --> 34:40.100
statistical variance

34:40.100 --> 34:41.420
don't even measure

34:41.420 --> 34:42.000
what's missing.

34:42.000 --> 34:43.580
What exactly are

34:43.580 --> 34:44.920
our current AI and

34:44.920 --> 34:45.880
statistical models

34:45.880 --> 34:47.100
actually optimizing

34:47.100 --> 34:47.440
for?

34:48.000 --> 34:48.500
This leads us

34:48.500 --> 34:49.500
directly to what

34:49.500 --> 34:50.240
might be the most

34:50.240 --> 34:51.280
devastating section

34:51.280 --> 34:51.860
of the paper,

34:52.060 --> 34:52.960
where the author

34:52.960 --> 34:54.060
takes aim at the

34:54.060 --> 34:54.840
most celebrated

34:54.840 --> 34:55.980
achievements of the

34:55.980 --> 34:57.040
modern tech industry.

34:57.280 --> 34:58.220
The proxy problem.

34:58.220 --> 34:59.160
The proxy problem.

34:59.480 --> 35:00.400
The central failure

35:00.400 --> 35:01.220
mode of modern

35:01.220 --> 35:02.100
intelligence frameworks

35:02.100 --> 35:03.140
is that we take a

35:03.140 --> 35:04.420
proxy metric, something

35:04.420 --> 35:05.180
like compression,

35:05.440 --> 35:06.040
prediction, or

35:06.040 --> 35:07.280
statistics, and we

35:07.280 --> 35:08.400
mistakenly elevate it

35:08.400 --> 35:09.380
to be the foundational

35:09.380 --> 35:10.300
definition of

35:10.300 --> 35:10.740
intelligence.

35:11.000 --> 35:11.940
We convince ourselves

35:11.940 --> 35:12.760
that the proxy is

35:12.760 --> 35:13.200
reality.

35:13.400 --> 35:13.660
Yes.

35:14.040 --> 35:14.840
And then we build

35:14.840 --> 35:15.880
systems that become

35:15.880 --> 35:17.100
phenomenally good at

35:17.100 --> 35:17.860
excelling at the

35:17.860 --> 35:19.040
proxy metric, while

35:19.040 --> 35:20.480
secretly, systematically

35:20.480 --> 35:21.440
destroying the

35:21.440 --> 35:22.160
underlying structure

35:22.160 --> 35:22.760
of the future.

35:22.760 --> 35:24.500
Fliction systematically

35:24.500 --> 35:25.720
dismantles four

35:25.720 --> 35:27.340
specific proxies that

35:27.340 --> 35:28.460
dominate our current

35:28.460 --> 35:29.640
intellectual landscape.

35:30.440 --> 35:31.100
Let's dive into the

35:31.100 --> 35:32.120
first one, because it's

35:32.120 --> 35:33.160
a direct attack on how

35:33.160 --> 35:33.920
we build artificial

35:33.920 --> 35:34.540
intelligence.

35:35.280 --> 35:37.080
Compression as

35:37.080 --> 35:38.240
controlled forgetting.

35:38.560 --> 35:40.200
This one is huge,

35:40.380 --> 35:41.280
because the entire

35:41.280 --> 35:42.820
modern history of AI is

35:42.820 --> 35:44.060
essentially an obsession

35:44.060 --> 35:44.940
with compression.

35:45.500 --> 35:45.820
Right.

35:46.180 --> 35:47.320
We worship Occam's

35:47.320 --> 35:48.720
razor, the idea that

35:48.720 --> 35:49.660
the simplest explanation

35:49.660 --> 35:50.360
is the best.

35:50.360 --> 35:51.260
We use minimum

35:51.260 --> 35:52.080
description length,

35:52.080 --> 35:53.680
and algorithmic

35:53.680 --> 35:54.520
information theory.

35:54.720 --> 35:55.640
The prevailing dogma

35:55.640 --> 35:56.820
is that true understanding

35:56.820 --> 35:58.280
is perfectly synonymous

35:58.280 --> 35:58.960
with compression.

35:59.740 --> 36:00.680
To understand a data

36:00.680 --> 36:01.580
set is to find the

36:01.580 --> 36:02.500
absolute shortest

36:02.500 --> 36:03.360
possible way to

36:03.360 --> 36:03.980
describe it.

36:04.040 --> 36:05.260
It sounds incredibly

36:05.260 --> 36:06.160
elegant in theory,

36:06.800 --> 36:07.900
but Fliction argues

36:07.900 --> 36:08.780
that this pressure to

36:08.780 --> 36:10.040
compress actively

36:10.040 --> 36:11.000
rewards the system

36:11.000 --> 36:11.860
for discarding the

36:11.860 --> 36:12.600
crack in the bridge.

36:12.720 --> 36:13.420
Because preserving

36:13.420 --> 36:14.080
the data of the

36:14.080 --> 36:15.200
crack barely saves

36:15.200 --> 36:16.080
any bits of memory.

36:16.360 --> 36:16.920
Exactly.

36:17.560 --> 36:18.440
Imagine a compression

36:18.440 --> 36:19.700
algorithm analyzing

36:19.700 --> 36:20.980
billions of data points

36:20.980 --> 36:21.540
flowing from the

36:21.540 --> 36:22.060
bridge's sensor.

36:22.080 --> 36:23.720
It sees massive,

36:23.940 --> 36:24.620
obvious patterns,

36:24.720 --> 36:25.620
the daily rhythm of

36:25.620 --> 36:26.860
traffic, the temperature

36:26.860 --> 36:27.760
fluctuations of the

36:27.760 --> 36:28.060
steel.

36:28.360 --> 36:29.480
It devotes memory to

36:29.480 --> 36:30.220
those because they

36:30.220 --> 36:31.480
describe the bulk of

36:31.480 --> 36:32.460
the bridge's behavior.

36:33.100 --> 36:34.080
Then it spots the

36:34.080 --> 36:35.180
microscopic anomaly

36:35.180 --> 36:35.880
of the crack.

36:36.300 --> 36:37.640
The algorithm calculates

36:37.640 --> 36:38.680
the trade-off and

36:38.680 --> 36:39.200
concludes,

36:39.640 --> 36:40.660
preserving this tiny

36:40.660 --> 36:41.960
distinction costs

36:41.960 --> 36:43.260
too much memory for

36:43.260 --> 36:44.740
almost zero payoff in

36:44.740 --> 36:45.460
summarizing the

36:45.460 --> 36:46.120
present data.

36:46.600 --> 36:48.200
So it averages it out.

36:48.460 --> 36:49.060
It forgets it.

36:49.060 --> 36:50.460
Compression is literally

36:50.460 --> 36:52.320
mathematically defined as

36:52.320 --> 36:53.360
the destruction of

36:53.360 --> 36:53.960
distinctions.

36:54.500 --> 36:55.620
But the problem is the

36:55.620 --> 36:56.900
demands of compression

36:56.900 --> 36:57.740
and the demands of

36:57.740 --> 36:59.400
admissibility frequently do

36:59.400 --> 37:00.300
not align at all.

37:00.700 --> 37:01.340
They are often

37:01.340 --> 37:02.680
diametrically opposed.

37:02.960 --> 37:03.680
The pressure of

37:03.680 --> 37:04.700
aggressive compression

37:04.700 --> 37:06.200
actively hunts down and

